==English==

===Etymology===
Arisen from {{m|en|clench}} in 16th century.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-clinch.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪntʃ|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|es}}

# To [[clasp]]; to [[interlock]]. {{defdate|1560s}}
# To make [[certain]]; to [[finalize]]. {{defdate|1716}}
#: ''I already planned to buy the car, but the color was what really '''clinched''' it for me.''
#* {{quote-journal
|date=October 29, 2011
|author=Neil Johnston
|title=Norwich 3 - 3 Blackburn
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/15413656.stm
|page=
|passage=Vincent Kompany was sent off after conceding a penalty that was converted by Stephen Hunt to give Wolves hope. But Adam Johnson's curling shot in stoppage time '''clinched''' the points.}}
# To [[fasten]] [[securely]] or [[permanently]].
# To [[bend]] and [[hammer]] the point of (a [[nail]]) so it cannot be removed. {{defdate|17th century}}
# To [[embrace]] passionately.
# To hold firmly; to [[clench]].
#* Dryden
#*: '''Clinch''' the pointed spear.
# To set closely together; to close tightly.
#: ''to '''clinch''' the teeth or the fist''
#: {{rfquotek|Jonathan Swift}}

====Translations====
{{trans-top|to clasp, to interlock}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|сда́вливать|n}}, {{t+|ru|сжима́ть|n}}, {{t+|ru|обнима́ть|n}}
{{trans-mid}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|es}}

# Any of several [[fastening]]s.
# The act or process of holding fast; that which serves to hold fast; a [[grip]] or [[grasp]].
#: ''to get a good '''clinch''' of an antagonist, or of a weapon''
#: ''to secure anything by a '''clinch'''''
# {{label|en|obsolete}} A [[pun]].
#: {{rfquotek|Alexander Pope}}
# {{label|en|nautical}} A [[hitch]] or [[bend]] by which a rope is made fast to the ring of an [[anchor]], or the [[breeching]] of a ship's gun to the [[ringbolt]]s.
# A passionate [[embrace]].

====Translations====
{{trans-top|any of several fastenings}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|зажи́м|m}}, {{t+|ru|ско́ба|f}}, {{t+|ru|заклёпка|f}}, {{t+|ru|кле́мма|f}}
{{trans-mid}}
{{trans-bottom}}

